From 3bbe8f1681c6c08da960d6769f8054b768fd3b67 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 01:28:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] backends/color: Select per-monitor target overrides as
 user declarations

A monitor configuration can now declare an output's target luminance
and choose its BT.2100 gamut source (MetaColorTargetOverride). Make the
colour device's target selection honour it, with its own provenance,
"user-declared", in the shared table that DisplayConfig reports.

Luminance, in BT.2100 only: the override is the first source, ahead of
the DisplayID 2 physical luminance and the CTA-861 static metadata. It
is taken as a whole, like those sources. It is usable when it declares
the peak and the effective range passes the same validation, against the
encoding's nominal reference white, so a raised reference luminance
factor does not remove it. Without a declared minimum, black stays at
the encoding's. An unusable override falls through. The override needs
no EDID, so it is considered before the no-EDID return.

Primaries, in BT.2100: auto keeps the DisplayID native chromaticities;
base-edid selects the base EDID colour characteristics as a user
declaration when they are usable, and otherwise falls through to
DisplayID; none is a valid request for no gamut target and suppresses
both. The native SDR mode keeps its own encoding gamut target with EDID
provenance, and the default SDR and forced-HDR modes keep the fallback,
whatever the override.

The configuration rejects invalid overrides before they reach an
output. The selector validates independently, so it stays correct for
any caller.

The new /wayland/display-targets/user-override case rewrites the
fixture output's EDID, connector type, HDR capabilities, forced HDR and
reference luminance factor, so it runs in its own invocation.

Link: TBD-MR
